1152
01:12:36,000 --> 01:12:38,000
♪ OSE DEMANDER UN AGNEAU ♪

1153
01:12:38,000 --> 01:12:42,000
♪ ET QU'IL OBTIENNE
ENCORE LA MERE... ♪

1154
01:12:42,000 --> 01:12:45,000
AND WHERE HAVE YOU BEEN?

1155
01:12:45,000 --> 01:12:46,000
BUSY.

1156
01:12:46,000 --> 01:12:49,000
♪ ...RENDEZ-LE MOI! ♪

1157
01:12:49,000 --> 01:12:52,000
♪ J'AI SON AMOUR... ♪

1158
01:12:52,000 --> 01:12:54,000
HAVE YOU SEEN MY GLOVES?

1159
01:12:54,000 --> 01:12:57,000
NO, I HAVEN'T.

1160
01:12:57,000 --> 01:13:02,000
♪ ...IL A MA FOI. ♪

1161
01:13:10,000 --> 01:13:13,000
Japp:
YOU COULDN'T ASK FOR
THE TRAIL TO BE MUCH FRESHER.

1162
01:13:13,000 --> 01:13:16,000
I HEARD THIS MORNING.

1163
01:13:16,000 --> 01:13:19,000
I'VE BEEN ALL OVER THE COUNTRY
ON THE OTHER THREE ROBBERIES.

1164
01:13:19,000 --> 01:13:22,000
THIS IS THE FIRST ONE
CLOSE TO LONDON.

1165
01:13:22,000 --> 01:13:24,000
TELL ME ABOUT THIS
MONSIEUR MARCUS HARDMAN.

1166
01:13:24,000 --> 01:13:27,000
HE'S A JEWELRY COLLECTOR.

1167
01:13:27,000 --> 01:13:30,000
SOMETHING OF A CELEBRITY
IN HIS OWN WAY.

1168
01:13:30,000 --> 01:13:32,000
THROWS A LOT OF PARTIES.

1169
01:13:32,000 --> 01:13:34,000
A COLLECTOR, DO YOU SAY?

1170
01:13:34,000 --> 01:13:36,000
YES. NATURAL TARGET,
I SUPPOSE.

1171
01:13:36,000 --> 01:13:39,000
AND YET IN OTHER CIRCUMSTANCES

1172
01:13:39,000 --> 01:13:44,000
PERHAPS ALSO
THE NATURAL SUSPECT.

1173
01:13:58,000 --> 01:14:01,000
THEY WERE IN THE GARDEN
WHEN IT HAPPENED.

1174
01:14:01,000 --> 01:14:02,000
SOME SORT OF CONCERT.

1175
01:14:02,000 --> 01:14:03,000
IN THE GARDEN?

1176
01:14:03,000 --> 01:14:05,000
THERE'S A MARQUEE.

1177
01:14:05,000 --> 01:14:06,000
SIR?

1178
01:14:06,000 --> 01:14:08,000
YOU WERE ON DUTY
LAST NIGHT?

1179
01:14:08,000 --> 01:14:09,000
YES, SIR.

1180
01:14:09,000 --> 01:14:11,000
TELL US WHAT
HAPPENED THEN.

1181
01:14:11,000 --> 01:14:13,000
I WAS ON MY BEAT, SIR.

1182
01:14:13,000 --> 01:14:15,000
I WALKED PAST HERE
ABOUT 10:30.

1183
01:14:15,000 --> 01:14:17,000
AND?

1184
01:14:17,000 --> 01:14:21,000
WELL, SIR, I DID SEE
SOMETHING-- A VAGRANT.

1185
01:14:21,000 --> 01:14:25,000
HE WAS GOING THE OTHER WAY,
DOWN THERE.

1186
01:14:25,000 --> 01:14:27,000
THAT VAGRANT COULD HAVE HAD

1187
01:14:27,000 --> 01:14:30,000
A £10,000 NECKLACE
IN HIS POCKET.

1188
01:14:30,000 --> 01:14:33,000
I WASN'T TO KNOW, SIR.

1189
01:14:33,000 --> 01:14:34,000
INDEED NOT.

1190
01:14:34,000 --> 01:14:36,000
THANK YOU, OFFICER.

1191
01:14:36,000 --> 01:14:41,000
YOU'VE BEEN MOST ASSIDUOUS
IN YOUR DUTIES.

1192
01:14:44,000 --> 01:14:45,000
Hardman:
IT'S JUST GHASTLY.

1193
01:14:45,000 --> 01:14:47,000
THE PUBLICITY
WILL DESTROY ME.

1194
01:14:47,000 --> 01:14:50,000
WHEN DID YOU DISCOVER
THE THEFT?

1195
01:14:50,000 --> 01:14:53,000
IT WAS THIS MORNING.

1196
01:14:53,000 --> 01:14:54,000
I GOT UP LATE AFTER
LAST NIGHT'S CONCERT.

1197
01:14:54,000 --> 01:14:57,000
WE HAD NACORA PLAYING,
AND KATHERINE BIRD.

1198
01:14:57,000 --> 01:14:59,000
I CAME INTO THE ROOM...

1199
01:14:59,000 --> 01:15:00,000
MY BEAUTIFUL
EMERALD NECKLACE.

1200
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:02,000
Japp:
BUT IT IS INSURED.

1201
01:15:02,000 --> 01:15:03,000
INSURED?

1202
01:15:03,000 --> 01:15:04,000
IS IT?

1203
01:15:04,000 --> 01:15:06,000
WELL, OF COURSE
IT'S INSURED.

1204
01:15:06,000 --> 01:15:08,000
THAT'S NOT THE POINT.

1205
01:15:08,000 --> 01:15:09,000
IT'S IRREPLACEABLE.

1206
01:15:09,000 --> 01:15:13,000
IT USED TO
BELONG TO CATHERINE
DE MEDICI, YOU KNOW.

1207
01:15:13,000 --> 01:15:18,000
IT'S THROUGH HERE.

1208
01:15:20,000 --> 01:15:24,000
MONSIEUR HARDMAN--

1209
01:15:24,000 --> 01:15:27,000
THIS WINDOW, IT IS KEPT
LOCKED, YES?

1210
01:15:27,000 --> 01:15:29,000
OF COURSE.

1211
01:15:29,000 --> 01:15:34,000
I'LL SHOW YOU
THE SAFE.

1212
01:15:34,000 --> 01:15:37,000
WHAT'S DIFFERENT
ABOUT THIS WINDOW?

1213
01:15:37,000 --> 01:15:39,000
IT LOOKS
LIKE THE OTHERS.

1214
01:15:39,000 --> 01:15:41,000
IT IS AT THIS POINT

1215
01:15:41,000 --> 01:15:45,000
THE CLIMBING PLANT OUTSIDE
ALMOST REACHES THE SILL

1216
01:15:45,000 --> 01:15:47,000
AND MONSIEUR HARDMAN
IS, I THINK

1217
01:15:47,000 --> 01:15:49,000
A LITTLE OVERCONFIDENT

1218
01:15:49,000 --> 01:15:52,000
ABOUT THE LATCHES.

1219
01:15:52,000 --> 01:15:55,000
Japp:
HAS ANYTHING BEEN TOUCHED?

1220
01:15:55,000 --> 01:15:57,000
Hardman:
NO, CHIEF INSPECTOR.

1221
01:15:57,000 --> 01:16:00,000
THIS IS JUST HOW IT WAS
WHEN I CAME IN.

1222
01:16:00,000 --> 01:16:05,000
THE SAFE WAS OPEN AND
THE EMERALDS WERE GONE.

1223
01:16:05,000 --> 01:16:08,000
SO WHAT'S THIS THEN?

1224
01:16:08,000 --> 01:16:10,000
IS THIS YOURS, MR. HARDMAN?

1225
01:16:10,000 --> 01:16:12,000
NO.

1226
01:16:12,000 --> 01:16:17,000
WHAT DO YOU MAKE
OF THIS, POIROT?

1227
01:16:20,000 --> 01:16:25,000
IT IS THE GLOVE OF A MAN.

1228
01:16:25,000 --> 01:16:30,000
A MAN WITH SMALL HANDS.

1229
01:16:31,000 --> 01:16:36,000
WHAT HAVE WE HERE?

1230
01:16:45,000 --> 01:16:48,000
A CIGARETTE CASE...

1231
01:16:48,000 --> 01:16:53,000
EMPTY.

1232
01:16:54,000 --> 01:16:56,000
WITH THE INITIALS "B.P."

1233
01:16:56,000 --> 01:16:59,000
IS THAT OF ANY SIGNIFICANCE
TO YOU, MONSIEUR HARDMAN?

1234
01:16:59,000 --> 01:17:02,000
WELL, YES,
I SUPPOSE IT
COULD BELONG TO...

1235
01:17:02,000 --> 01:17:05,000
BERNARD PARKER.

1236
01:17:05,000 --> 01:17:06,000
A YOUNG FRIEND
OF MINE.

1237
01:17:06,000 --> 01:17:08,000
UH... A BUSINESS
ASSOCIATE.

1238
01:17:08,000 --> 01:17:10,000
THE GLOVE COULD
BE HIS, TOO.

1239
01:17:10,000 --> 01:17:11,000
HOW SO?

1240
01:17:11,000 --> 01:17:13,000
WELL, HE'D LOST
BOTH HIS GLOVES.

1241
01:17:13,000 --> 01:17:16,000
AT LEAST, THAT'S
WHAT HE SAID.

1242
01:17:16,000 --> 01:17:17,000
( doorbell )

1243
01:17:17,000 --> 01:17:21,000
EXCUSE ME.

1244
01:17:21,000 --> 01:17:22,000
SO WHAT DO YOU THINK, POIROT?

1245
01:17:22,000 --> 01:17:24,000
I THINK THAT IT IS STRANGE.

1246
01:17:24,000 --> 01:17:26,000
THE GLOVE AND
THE CIGARETTE CASE

1247
01:17:26,000 --> 01:17:29,000
IT IS, AS YOU MIGHT SAY,
THE DOUBLE CLUE.

1248
01:17:29,000 --> 01:17:32,000
TWICE AS MUCH TO WORK ON.

1249
01:17:32,000 --> 01:17:34,000
IT IS TOO MUCH,
HASTINGS, TOO MUCH.

1250
01:17:34,000 --> 01:17:37,000
TO LEAVE THE GLOVE
INSIDE THE SAFE...

1251
01:17:37,000 --> 01:17:39,000
Japp:
PERHAPS HE WAS IN A HURRY.

1252
01:17:39,000 --> 01:17:44,000
SOMEONE COULD HAVE
DISTURBED HIM.

1253
01:17:45,000 --> 01:17:46,000
POIROT?

1254
01:17:46,000 --> 01:17:48,000
Hardman:
MAY I INTRODUCE YOU?

1255
01:17:48,000 --> 01:17:50,000
COUNTESS
VERA ROSSAKOFF.

1256
01:17:50,000 --> 01:17:55,000
SHE WAS HERE
AT THE PARTY
LAST NIGHT.

1257
01:17:59,000 --> 01:18:01,000
ENCHANTE, MADAME.

1258
01:18:01,000 --> 01:18:02,000
( clicks heels together )

1259
01:18:02,000 --> 01:18:07,000
ET MOI AUSSI, MONSIEUR.

1260
01:18:07,000 --> 01:18:10,000
HERCULE POIROT.

1261
01:18:10,000 --> 01:18:15,000
AT YOUR SERVICE, MADAME.

1262
01:18:20,000 --> 01:18:24,000
Countess:
WHAT BEAUTIFUL GARDENS,
MONSIEUR POIROT.

1263
01:18:24,000 --> 01:18:26,000
IT IS SO GOOD OF YOU
TO ESCORT ME.

1264
01:18:26,000 --> 01:18:30,000
Poirot:
I WANTED TO BRING YOU SOMEWHERE
THAT WAS WORTHY OF YOU.

1265
01:18:30,000 --> 01:18:34,000
Countess:
YOU MUSTN'T FLATTER ME,
YOU WILL MAKE ME CRY.

1266
01:18:34,000 --> 01:18:37,000
I'VE BEEN SO LONELY
SINCE I ARRIVED

1267
01:18:37,000 --> 01:18:39,000
AND IT'S BEEN A LOVELY DAY.

1268
01:18:39,000 --> 01:18:41,000
YES, I ALSO KNOW
WHAT IT IS

1269
01:18:41,000 --> 01:18:43,000
TO COME ALONE
TO A NEW COUNTRY.

1270
01:18:43,000 --> 01:18:46,000
OF COURSE--
YOU YOURSELF
ARE A REFUGEE.

1271
01:18:46,000 --> 01:18:50,000
YES, I WAS HEAD OF
THE POLICE IN BELGIUM.

1272
01:18:50,000 --> 01:18:51,000
AH-HA.

1273
01:18:51,000 --> 01:18:53,000
BUT THEN CAME
THE GREAT WAR.

1274
01:18:53,000 --> 01:18:55,000
I WAS FORCED TO LEAVE.

1275
01:18:55,000 --> 01:18:57,000
I CAME TO ENGLAND

1276
01:18:57,000 --> 01:18:59,000
TO THE VILLAGE
OF STYLES ST. MARY.

1277
01:18:59,000 --> 01:19:02,000
BUT IT WAS A SAD AND
PAINFUL TIME FOR ME

1278
01:19:02,000 --> 01:19:05,000
SO FAR FROM MY FAMILY
AND MY HOME.

1279
01:19:05,000 --> 01:19:07,000
POOR MONSIEUR POIROT.

1280
01:19:07,000 --> 01:19:09,000
OH, NO, I HAVE HAD
THE GOOD FORTUNE.

1281
01:19:09,000 --> 01:19:11,000
MY TALENT, IT HAS
BEEN RECOGNIZED.

1282
01:19:11,000 --> 01:19:14,000
IT IS REPORTED

1283
01:19:14,000 --> 01:19:18,000
THAT I AM THE MOST FAMOUS
DETECTIVE IN ENGLAND.

1284
01:19:18,000 --> 01:19:19,000
POIROT HERE?

1285
01:19:19,000 --> 01:19:20,000
NO, I'M AFRAID...

1286
01:19:20,000 --> 01:19:21,000
HASTINGS?

1287
01:19:21,000 --> 01:19:22,000
YES.

1288
01:19:22,000 --> 01:19:27,000
EXCUSE ME.

1289
01:19:28,000 --> 01:19:29,000
WHERE IS HE?

1290
01:19:29,000 --> 01:19:30,000
ARE YOU ALL RIGHT?

1291
01:19:30,000 --> 01:19:31,000
NO, I AM NOT ALL RIGHT.

1292
01:19:31,000 --> 01:19:34,000
I'M ABOUT TO GET THE SACK.

1293
01:19:34,000 --> 01:19:35,000
AS BAD AS THAT?

1294
01:19:35,000 --> 01:19:37,000
IT'S BEEN THREE DAYS,
CAPTAIN HASTINGS.

1295
01:19:37,000 --> 01:19:39,000
THREE DAYS AND NOTHING.

1296
01:19:39,000 --> 01:19:42,000
POIROT IS ON
THE CASE, YOU KNOW.

1297
01:19:42,000 --> 01:19:44,000
THEN WHY AREN'T
YOU WITH HIM?

1298
01:19:44,000 --> 01:19:45,000
I DON'T KNOW.

1299
01:19:45,000 --> 01:19:47,000
I SUPPOSE HE WANTED
TO PURSUE

1300
01:19:47,000 --> 01:19:49,000
HIS OWN LINE OF ENQUIRY.

1301
01:19:49,000 --> 01:19:52,000
THIS JEWELRY BUSINESS
WILL RUIN ME.

1302
01:19:52,000 --> 01:19:53,000
DON'T WORRY, OLD CHAP.

1303
01:19:53,000 --> 01:19:56,000
YOU KNOW POIROT.

1304
01:19:56,000 --> 01:19:58,000
HE REALLY IS
GETTING SOMEWHERE?

1305
01:19:58,000 --> 01:20:03,000
OH, YES, I'M SURE HE'S
ON THE RIGHT TRACK.

1306
01:20:06,000 --> 01:20:09,000
AND SO YOU ARE
INVESTIGATING

1307
01:20:09,000 --> 01:20:11,000
THE CASE OF
THE STOLEN JEWELRY?

1308
01:20:11,000 --> 01:20:14,000
IT SEEMS A SIMPLE
MATTER, MADAME.

1309
01:20:14,000 --> 01:20:17,000
TO ENGLAND'S MOST FAMOUS
DETECTIVE, OF COURSE.

1310
01:20:17,000 --> 01:20:19,000
DO YOU SUSPECT ME?

1311
01:20:19,000 --> 01:20:20,000
SHOULD I?

1312
01:20:20,000 --> 01:20:22,000
I WAS THERE.

1313
01:20:22,000 --> 01:20:25,000
THAT IS TRUE.

1314
01:20:25,000 --> 01:20:26,000
SO TELL ME, MADAME

1315
01:20:26,000 --> 01:20:29,000
WHY DID YOU RETURN
THE FOLLOWING DAY?

1316
01:20:29,000 --> 01:20:33,000
I HAD AN APPOINTMENT
WITH MR. HARDMAN.

1317
01:20:33,000 --> 01:20:34,000
I WAS CONSIDERING

1318
01:20:34,000 --> 01:20:39,000
SELLING SOME OF
MY OWN JEWELRY.

1319
01:20:46,000 --> 01:20:51,000
I HAD A HOUSE LIKE THIS
ONCE IN PETROGRAD.

1320
01:20:52,000 --> 01:20:56,000
FIRST A HOUSE.

1321
01:20:56,000 --> 01:20:59,000
THEN A WING
IN THE HOUSE...

1322
01:20:59,000 --> 01:21:02,000
THEN A ROOM
IN THE WING...

1323
01:21:02,000 --> 01:21:04,000
AND NOW NOTHING.

1324
01:21:04,000 --> 01:21:07,000
BUT THAT DOES NOT MAKE YOU
THE THIEF, MADAME.

1325
01:21:07,000 --> 01:21:12,000
NO.

1326
01:21:12,000 --> 01:21:15,000
LIFE ITSELF
IS THE THIEF.

1327
01:21:15,000 --> 01:21:20,000
IT HAS TAKEN
EVERYTHING I HAVE.

1328
01:21:39,000 --> 01:21:43,000
WELL, I SUPPOSE IT HAD
TO HAPPEN ONE DAY.

1329
01:21:43,000 --> 01:21:46,000
HE'S REALLY QUITE
TAKEN, YOU KNOW.

1330
01:21:46,000 --> 01:21:49,000
ARE YOU SURE,
CAPTAIN HASTINGS?

1331
01:21:49,000 --> 01:21:51,000
YOU SHOULD HAVE
SEEN HIM.

1332
01:21:51,000 --> 01:21:53,000
WELL, ANYWAY, WE
HAVEN'T SEEN HIM--

1333
01:21:53,000 --> 01:21:55,000
NOT FOR THREE DAYS.

1334
01:21:55,000 --> 01:21:56,000
IT'S NOT LIKE HIM.

1335
01:21:56,000 --> 01:21:58,000
WHAT ABOUT
POOR OLD JAPP?

1336
01:21:58,000 --> 01:22:01,000
LOOKS LIKE HE COULD
BE FOR THE CHOP.

1337
01:22:01,000 --> 01:22:04,000
IF ONLY WE COULD HELP.

1338
01:22:04,000 --> 01:22:06,000

MAYBE WE CAN.

1339
01:22:06,000 --> 01:22:07,000
HOW?

1340
01:22:07,000 --> 01:22:09,000
ONLY FOUR PEOPLE
WENT INTO THE HOUSE

1341
01:22:09,000 --> 01:22:12,000
DURING THE CONCERT.

1342
01:22:12,000 --> 01:22:13,000
THAT'S WHEN THE JEWELS
WERE TAKEN.

1343
01:22:13,000 --> 01:22:16,000
BERNARD PARKER,
MARTIN JOHNSTONE

1344
01:22:16,000 --> 01:22:18,000
LADY RUNCORN
AND THE COUNTESS.

1345
01:22:18,000 --> 01:22:20,000
SUPPOSE IT WAS HER?

1346
01:22:20,000 --> 01:22:22,000
OH, IT CAN'T
HAVE BEEN HER.

1347
01:22:22,000 --> 01:22:24,000
I MEAN, I HOPE
TO GOD IT WASN'T.

1348
01:22:24,000 --> 01:22:25,000
SO WHAT CAN WE DO?

1349
01:22:25,000 --> 01:22:27,000
WE'LL TALK
TO THOSE THREE

1350
01:22:27,000 --> 01:22:31,000
AND SOLVE THIS
OURSELVES.

1351
01:22:31,000 --> 01:22:36,000
Hastings:
I KNOW OLD JAPP SUSPECTS
THAT TRAMP THE CONSTABLE SPOTTED

1352
01:22:36,000 --> 01:22:38,000
BUT I THINK WE SHOULD
SPEAK TO THEM ALL

1353
01:22:38,000 --> 01:22:43,000
STARTING WITH MR. JOHNSTONE.

1354
01:22:43,000 --> 01:22:45,000
Johnstone:
HOW DARE YOU

1355
01:22:45,000 --> 01:22:46,000
SIR!

1356
01:22:46,000 --> 01:22:49,000
IF I WANT A JEWEL NECKLACE

1357
01:22:49,000 --> 01:22:53,000
I CAN AFFORD TO GO
INTO A SHOP AND BUY ONE.

1358
01:22:53,000 --> 01:22:57,000
IN FACT, I CAN AFFORD
TO BUY THE SHOP!

1359
01:22:57,000 --> 01:23:00,000
SO WHY DID YOU GO
BACK INTO THE HOUSE?

1360
01:23:00,000 --> 01:23:05,000
A CALL OF NATURE--
IF YOU MUST KNOW.

1361
01:23:05,000 --> 01:23:10,000
WERE THERE ANY WITNESSES?

1362
01:23:12,000 --> 01:23:17,000
( door slams )

1363
01:23:17,000 --> 01:23:18,000
SO, LADY RUNCORN

1364
01:23:18,000 --> 01:23:22,000
YOU DON'T DENY THAT YOU
WENT BACK INTO THE HOUSE.

1365
01:23:22,000 --> 01:23:25,000
BUT I JUST TOLD YOU
THAT, CAPTAIN HASTINGS.

1366
01:23:25,000 --> 01:23:28,000
HMM, DID YOU SEE
ANYONE ELSE INSIDE?

1367
01:23:28,000 --> 01:23:30,000
YES, THERE WAS SOMEONE

1368
01:23:30,000 --> 01:23:32,000
COMING OUT OF THE
DOWNSTAIRS BATHROOM

1369
01:23:32,000 --> 01:23:35,000
BUT I DIDN'T SEE
WHO IT WAS.

1370
01:23:35,000 --> 01:23:39,000
WHY WERE YOU IN THE HOUSE,
LADY RUNCORN?

1371
01:23:39,000 --> 01:23:42,000
I WENT TO FETCH MY HANDBAG.

1372
01:23:42,000 --> 01:23:47,000
I'D LEFT IT INSIDE.

1373
01:23:49,000 --> 01:23:51,000
Poirot:
EH BIEN, COUNTESS.

1374
01:23:51,000 --> 01:23:54,000
TOMORROW I WILL TAKE YOU
TO THE TATE GALLERY

1375
01:23:54,000 --> 01:23:57,000
TO SEE THEIR COLLECTION
OF THE TURNERS.

1376
01:23:57,000 --> 01:24:01,000
Countess:
BUT I FIND THESE EXPRESSIONIST
PAINTERS SO EXCITING.

1377
01:24:01,000 --> 01:24:03,000
DON'T YOU, HERCULE?

1378
01:24:03,000 --> 01:24:04,000
IN PART, YES.

1379
01:24:04,000 --> 01:24:07,000
DO YOU KNOW,
I DO NOT BELIEVE

1380
01:24:07,000 --> 01:24:10,000
THAT THERE IS ANYONE
OF MY ACQUAINTANCE

1381
01:24:10,000 --> 01:24:12,000
WHO CALLS ME
BY THAT NAME.

1382
01:24:12,000 --> 01:24:14,000
OH, I'M SO SORRY.

1383
01:24:14,000 --> 01:24:16,000
EXCUSE ME.

1384
01:24:16,000 --> 01:24:20,000
MADAME.

1385
01:24:20,000 --> 01:24:24,000
NOW THIS I FIND
MOST PLEASING.

1386
01:24:24,000 --> 01:24:27,000
MARC CHAGALL, ANOTHER
RUSSIAN, AN EXILE.

1387
01:24:27,000 --> 01:24:30,000
HE LIVES IN PARIS,
I BELIEVE.

1388
01:24:30,000 --> 01:24:33,000
A CONSTRUCTIVIST,
THEY CALL HIM.

1389
01:24:33,000 --> 01:24:37,000
BUT ONE ONLY HAS TO LOOK
AT HIS WORK TO SEE.

1390
01:24:37,000 --> 01:24:39,000
YES?

1391
01:24:39,000 --> 01:24:42,000
HE'S STILL IN RUSSIA,
HE NEVER LEFT.

1392
01:24:42,000 --> 01:24:44,000
LIKE YOU, COUNTESS.

1393
01:24:44,000 --> 01:24:46,000
OH, NO.

1394
01:24:46,000 --> 01:24:51,000
IT'S ANOTHER WORLD
NOW-- STALIN, HITLER.

1395
01:24:51,000 --> 01:24:55,000
EVERYWHERE YOU LOOK
IT'S ALL CHANGING.

1396
01:24:55,000 --> 01:25:00,000
( sighs )

1397
01:25:02,000 --> 01:25:06,000
GOOD-BYE, LADY RUNCORN.

1398
01:25:06,000 --> 01:25:11,000
( annoyed: )
GOOD-BYE, CAPTAIN HASTINGS.

1399
01:25:12,000 --> 01:25:15,000
CAPTAIN HASTINGS, LOOK.

1400
01:25:15,000 --> 01:25:17,000
"B.P."

1401
01:25:17,000 --> 01:25:22,000
BERNARD PARKER!

1402
01:25:22,000 --> 01:25:27,000
YES, I DO SOME WORK
FOR MARCUS-- MR. HARDMAN.

1403
01:25:27,000 --> 01:25:31,000
I ARRANGE HIS MORE
INSALUBRIOUS DEALS.

1404
01:25:31,000 --> 01:25:33,000
WHAT DO YOU MEAN
BY THAT?

1405
01:25:33,000 --> 01:25:36,000
WHEN THE CLIENT DOESN'T WANT

1406
01:25:36,000 --> 01:25:38,000
HIS OR HER NAME DRAGGED
THROUGH THE MUD

1407
01:25:38,000 --> 01:25:40,000
I ACT AS A SORT
OF GO-BETWEEN.

1408
01:25:40,000 --> 01:25:41,000
I DON'T, UH...

1409
01:25:41,000 --> 01:25:43,000
IN HIGH SOCIETY,
CAPTAIN

1410
01:25:43,000 --> 01:25:45,000
NOBODY IS EVER HARD UP.

1411
01:25:45,000 --> 01:25:48,000
AT LEAST, THEY DON'T
ADMIT TO IT.

1412
01:25:48,000 --> 01:25:51,000
SO WHEN THEY ARE HARD UP
THEY COME TO ME

1413
01:25:51,000 --> 01:25:53,000
AND I GO TO MARCUS.

1414
01:25:53,000 --> 01:25:55,000
HAVE YOU LOST A GLOVE?

1415
01:25:55,000 --> 01:25:57,000
OH... THE GLOVE!

1416
01:25:57,000 --> 01:25:59,000
THAT SIMPLY LOATHSOME
INSPECTOR SNAPP

1417
01:25:59,000 --> 01:26:02,000
HAS ALREADY BEEN ONTO ME
ABOUT THE GLOVE.

1418
01:26:02,000 --> 01:26:05,000
DO YOU KNOW, HE WANTED
TO ARREST ME.

1419
01:26:05,000 --> 01:26:07,000
WAS IT YOURS?

1420
01:26:07,000 --> 01:26:08,000
YES.

1421
01:26:08,000 --> 01:26:11,000
BUT I'VE TOLD HIM--
I HAD BOTH MY GLOVES

1422
01:26:11,000 --> 01:26:12,000
WHEN THE CONCERT BEGAN

1423
01:26:12,000 --> 01:26:15,000
AND THAT WAS THE LAST TIME
I SAW THEM.

1424
01:26:15,000 --> 01:26:18,000
AND I'VE NEVER SEEN
THAT CIGARETTE CASE BEFORE.

1425
01:26:18,000 --> 01:26:19,000
IT HAD YOUR INITIALS.

1426
01:26:19,000 --> 01:26:22,000
OH, MY DEAR--
I'M SURE YOU'LL FIND

1427
01:26:22,000 --> 01:26:26,000
PLENTY OF PEOPLE IN LONDON
WITH THE INITIALS "B.P."

1428
01:26:26,000 --> 01:26:27,000
BY THE WAY

1429
01:26:27,000 --> 01:26:30,000
HAVE YOU EVER DONE
ANY EMBROIDERY?

1430
01:26:30,000 --> 01:26:32,000
EMBROIDERY?

1431
01:26:32,000 --> 01:26:35,000
NEEDLEWORK, THAT SORT OF THING.

1432
01:26:35,000 --> 01:26:37,000
NO.

1433
01:26:37,000 --> 01:26:40,000
( laughs )

1434
01:26:40,000 --> 01:26:45,000

NO!

1435
01:26:46,000 --> 01:26:50,000
AND WHAT OF
THE PERFECT CRIME?

1436
01:26:50,000 --> 01:26:53,000
IT IS AN ILLUSION,
COUNTESS.

1437
01:26:53,000 --> 01:26:54,000
MAYBE.

1438
01:26:54,000 --> 01:26:57,000
BUT LET ME TELL YOU
SOMETHING I HEARD.

1439
01:26:57,000 --> 01:27:01,000
IT WAS A ROBBERY, LIKE
THE ONE AT MR. HARDMAN'S

1440
01:27:01,000 --> 01:27:04,000
BUT THIS TIME
IT WAS DIAMONDS.

1441
01:27:04,000 --> 01:27:06,000
PLEASE GO ON.

1442
01:27:06,000 --> 01:27:07,000
12 PEOPLE SAT DOWN
TO DINNER.

1443
01:27:07,000 --> 01:27:10,000
THE SAFE WAS
IN THE NEXT ROOM.

1444
01:27:10,000 --> 01:27:11,000
THE HOSTESS CHECKED

1445
01:27:11,000 --> 01:27:13,000
THAT THE DIAMONDS
WERE IN PLACE

1446
01:27:13,000 --> 01:27:15,000
BEFORE THE MEAL BEGAN.

1447
01:27:15,000 --> 01:27:16,000
AND AFTER?

1448
01:27:16,000 --> 01:27:21,000
THE SAFE WAS OPEN,
AND EMPTY.

1449
01:27:21,000 --> 01:27:22,000
THERE WERE NO CLUES?

1450
01:27:22,000 --> 01:27:25,000
NONE.

1451
01:27:25,000 --> 01:27:26,000
IT IS STRANGE THAT
YOU TALK OF THIS.

1452
01:27:26,000 --> 01:27:27,000
I ALSO HAVE READ OF IT.

1453
01:27:27,000 --> 01:27:28,000
YES?

1454
01:27:28,000 --> 01:27:31,000
OH, YES--
THE ATTERTON DIAMONDS.

1455
01:27:31,000 --> 01:27:34,000
THE POLICE WERE CONFOUNDED
BECAUSE THEY LOOK

1456
01:27:34,000 --> 01:27:36,000
WITH THE EYES
THAT IS TOO... ENGLISH.

1457
01:27:36,000 --> 01:27:38,000
I DON'T UNDERSTAND.

1458
01:27:38,000 --> 01:27:41,000
THEY SEE THE DUKES
AND THE DUCHESSES

1459
01:27:41,000 --> 01:27:43,000
BUT THEY FAIL
TO ASK THEMSELVES

1460
01:27:43,000 --> 01:27:44,000
THE ONE SIMPLE QUESTION.

1461
01:27:44,000 --> 01:27:46,000
WHICH IS?

1462
01:27:46,000 --> 01:27:48,000
WHAT PERSON WAS AT THAT DINNER

1463
01:27:48,000 --> 01:27:51,000
WHO WAS ALSO AT THE SOIREE
OF MONSIEUR HARDMAN

1464
01:27:51,000 --> 01:27:56,000
AND ON TWO OTHER OCCASIONS
WHEN SOME JEWELS WERE STOLEN?

1465
01:27:56,000 --> 01:27:59,000
SO YOU'RE SAYING THE POLICE
CANNOT SOLVE IT

1466
01:27:59,000 --> 01:28:03,000
BECAUSE THEY'RE TOO ENGLISH.

1467
01:28:03,000 --> 01:28:08,000
OR BECAUSE THE CULPRIT,
HE IS NOT ENGLISH ENOUGH.

1468
01:28:17,000 --> 01:28:20,000
THANK YOU FOR
A REALLY LOVELY DAY.

1469
01:28:20,000 --> 01:28:25,000
COUNTESS.

1470
01:28:36,000 --> 01:28:38,000
WILL I SEE YOU TOMORROW?

1471
01:28:38,000 --> 01:28:42,000
POUR LE DEJEUNER? OUI.

1472
01:28:42,000 --> 01:28:47,000
THE CARLTON HOTEL.

1473
01:28:58,000 --> 01:29:02,000
BERNARD PARKER MUST
HAVE BEEN IN LOVE
WITH LADY RUNCORN.

1474
01:29:02,000 --> 01:29:05,000
HE GAVE HER THE EMBROIDERY,
BUT LIED ABOUT IT TO US.

1475
01:29:05,000 --> 01:29:08,000
I THINK JOHNSTONE
WAS LYING AS WELL.

1476
01:29:08,000 --> 01:29:09,000
I'M SURE IT'S HIM.

1477
01:29:09,000 --> 01:29:12,000
( Poirot practicing vowels )

1478
01:29:12,000 --> 01:29:14,000
I SAY, POIROT!

1479
01:29:14,000 --> 01:29:16,000
LEARNING RUSSIAN!

1480
01:29:16,000 --> 01:29:19,000
I WONDER WHY?

1481
01:29:19,000 --> 01:29:22,000
IT HAS BEEN
AN INVALUABLE AID, MON AMI.

1482
01:29:22,000 --> 01:29:25,000
I COULDN'T GET PAST
THE ALPHABET.

1483
01:29:25,000 --> 01:29:27,000
NO, IT IS INDEED
COMPLICATED.

1484
01:29:27,000 --> 01:29:28,000
YES-- LIKE THIS CASE.

1485
01:29:28,000 --> 01:29:29,000
THE STOLEN JEWELS?

1486
01:29:29,000 --> 01:29:32,000
WE'VE BEEN INVESTIGATING,
MR. POIROT.

1487
01:29:32,000 --> 01:29:36,000
YOU'RE NOT THE ONLY
PRIVATE DETECTIVE
IN LONDON, YOU KNOW.

1488
01:29:36,000 --> 01:29:41,000
THIS IS TRUE, MON AMI.

1489
01:29:41,000 --> 01:29:46,000
EH BIEN, TELL ME ALL
THAT YOU HAVE DISCOVERED.

1490
01:29:49,000 --> 01:29:52,000
SO WE THINK BERNARD PARKER
IS BLACKMAILING LADY RUNCORN.

1491
01:29:52,000 --> 01:29:54,000
Lemon:
SHE HAS HUGE DEBTS

1492
01:29:54,000 --> 01:29:57,000
AND SHE'S SENT FOUR
PAINTINGS TO AUCTION.

1493
01:29:57,000 --> 01:30:00,000
SHE MUST HAVE SOLD THEM
TO PAY HIM OFF.

1494
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:03,000
SO YOU SAY
LADY RUNCORN
REQUIRES MONEY?

1495
01:30:03,000 --> 01:30:06,000
Hastings:
YES.

1496
01:30:06,000 --> 01:30:09,000
THAT LADY RUNCORN
WOULD MEET WITH MONSIEUR PARKER

1497
01:30:09,000 --> 01:30:11,000
TO DISCUSS
THE SELLING OF HER JEWELS?

1498
01:30:11,000 --> 01:30:13,000
THAT IS HIS
OCCUPATION,
IS IT NOT?

1499
01:30:13,000 --> 01:30:15,000
BUT WHAT ABOUT
THE EMBROIDERY?

1500
01:30:15,000 --> 01:30:16,000
HE SIGNED IT "B.P."

1501
01:30:16,000 --> 01:30:20,000
BUT THEN, I WONDER WHAT
LADY RUNCORN WAS CALLED

1502
01:30:20,000 --> 01:30:22,000
BEFORE SHE BECAME LADY RUNCORN?

1503
01:30:22,000 --> 01:30:24,000
I HADN'T
THOUGHT OF THAT.

1504
01:30:24,000 --> 01:30:26,000
IF YOU ARE TO BE
HERCULE POIROT

1505
01:30:26,000 --> 01:30:31,000
YOU MUST THINK
OF EVERYTHING.

1506
01:30:37,000 --> 01:30:42,000
"LADY RUNCORN,
BORN BEATRICE PALMERSTONE."

1507
01:30:43,000 --> 01:30:45,000
B.P.--

1508
01:30:45,000 --> 01:30:50,000
HE'S RIGHT.

1509
01:30:52,000 --> 01:30:55,000
SO, ALL RIGHT THEN, POIROT,
WHAT DO YOU THINK?

1510
01:30:55,000 --> 01:30:58,000
I THINK YOU MUST ASK
YOURSELF ONE QUESTION:

1511
01:30:58,000 --> 01:30:59,000
YES?

1512
01:30:59,000 --> 01:31:00,000
THE TRAMP--

1513
01:31:00,000 --> 01:31:02,000
WHAT WAS HE
DOING THERE?

1514
01:31:02,000 --> 01:31:03,000
AND THE ANSWER?

1515
01:31:03,000 --> 01:31:06,000
YOU WILL FIND
BY RETURNING TO THE HOME

1516
01:31:06,000 --> 01:31:07,000
OF MONSIEUR HARDMAN.

1517
01:31:07,000 --> 01:31:09,000
I SUGGEST YOU GO TOMORROW.

1518
01:31:09,000 --> 01:31:10,000
WHAT ABOUT YOU?

1519
01:31:10,000 --> 01:31:13,000
THIS CASE DOES
NOT INTEREST POIROT.

1520
01:31:13,000 --> 01:31:15,000
I HAVE NO ENTHUSIASM FOR IT.

1521
01:31:15,000 --> 01:31:18,000
AND IT IS, AS YOU
YOURSELF SAY, HASTINGS

1522
01:31:18,000 --> 01:31:20,000
THERE ARE OTHER
PRIVATE DETECTIVES.

1523
01:31:20,000 --> 01:31:23,000
YES, I'M SURE.

1524
01:31:23,000 --> 01:31:25,000
WHAT ABOUT POOR OLD JAPP?

1525
01:31:25,000 --> 01:31:30,000
OH.

1526
01:31:30,000 --> 01:31:33,000
Japp:
NO, SIR, NOTHING AS YET, SIR.

1527
01:31:33,000 --> 01:31:36,000
BUT I AM FOLLOWING UP
A FEW LEADS.

1528
01:31:36,000 --> 01:31:40,000
UH, WELL, NO, SIR, THEY HAVEN'T
ACTUALLY LED ANYWHERE YET.

1529
01:31:40,000 --> 01:31:45,000
BUT IF YOU COULD JUST GIVE ME
A LITTLE BIT MORE TIME...

1530
01:31:45,000 --> 01:31:48,000
48 HOURS?

1531
01:31:48,000 --> 01:31:53,000
I WAS HOPING FOR A LITTLE BIT
MORE TIME THAN THAT, SIR.

1532
01:31:53,000 --> 01:31:58,000
I CAN ASSURE YOU, SIR,
YOUR WIFE IS PERFECTLY SAFE.

1533
01:31:58,000 --> 01:32:03,000
IF YOU'D LIKE TO PUT HER ON,
I CAN ASSURE HER MYSELF, SIR.

1534
01:32:03,000 --> 01:32:07,000
NO, SIR, I DIDN'T
MEAN THAT, SIR.

1535
01:32:07,000 --> 01:32:11,000
RIGHT, SIR.

1536
01:32:11,000 --> 01:32:13,000
YES, SIR.

1537
01:32:13,000 --> 01:32:16,000
I AM WORKING ON IT, SIR.

1538
01:32:16,000 --> 01:32:21,000
I'LL DO THAT, SIR.

1539
01:32:30,000 --> 01:32:35,000
THANK YOU.

1540
01:32:35,000 --> 01:32:37,000
GOOD EVENING.

1541
01:32:37,000 --> 01:32:42,000
PLEASE, ALLOW ME
TO INTRODUCE MYSELF.

1542
01:32:47,000 --> 01:32:49,000
POIROT!

1543
01:32:49,000 --> 01:32:50,000
NOT THE HERCULE POIROT?

1544
01:32:50,000 --> 01:32:51,000
THE VERY SAME.

1545
01:32:51,000 --> 01:32:53,000
PLEASE,
SIT DOWN, SIR.

1546
01:32:53,000 --> 01:32:55,000
NO, THANK YOU.

1547
01:32:55,000 --> 01:32:57,000
WHAT CAN WE DO FOR YOU?

1548
01:32:57,000 --> 01:33:00,000
I HAVE A FRIEND
WHO REQUIRES MY
IMMEDIATE HELP

1549
01:33:00,000 --> 01:33:03,000
BUT I CANNOT HELP,
AND SO I TURN TO YOU.

1550
01:33:03,000 --> 01:33:05,000
YOU MEAN YOU WANT US

1551
01:33:05,000 --> 01:33:07,000
TO SORT OF DEPUTIZE
FOR YOU?

1552
01:33:07,000 --> 01:33:10,000
OUI, C'EST CA,
YOU MUST ACT ON MY BEHALF.

1553
01:33:10,000 --> 01:33:12,000
IT IS MUST BE
SOMETHING IMPORTANT

1554
01:33:12,000 --> 01:33:15,000
FOR YOU TO ASK
FOR OUR HELP,
MONSIEUR POIROT.

1555
01:33:15,000 --> 01:33:18,000
IT IS A MATTER OF THE UTMOST
IMPORTANCE, MONSIEUR.

1556
01:33:18,000 --> 01:33:20,000
THERE IS A REPUTATION...

1557
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:24,000
NO, THERE IS A LIFE
THAT IS AT STAKE.

1558
01:33:24,000 --> 01:33:29,000
BUT HERCULE POIROT
IS NOT INVOLVED.

1559
01:33:33,000 --> 01:33:35,000
Hastings:
WHAT WAS THE TRAMP
DOING HERE?

1560
01:33:35,000 --> 01:33:38,000
I CAN'T SEE HOW WANDERING
AROUND THE GROUNDS

1561
01:33:38,000 --> 01:33:40,000
IS GOING TO GIVE
US THE ANSWER.

1562
01:33:40,000 --> 01:33:43,000
Lemon:
LET'S BE METHODICAL,
CAPTAIN HASTINGS.

1563
01:33:43,000 --> 01:33:46,000
MR. POIROT MUST
HAVE A REASON FOR
SENDING US HERE.

1564
01:33:46,000 --> 01:33:49,000
FOUR PEOPLE WENT
INTO THAT HOUSE.

1565
01:33:49,000 --> 01:33:51,000
ONE OF THEM
MUST HAVE DONE IT.

1566
01:33:51,000 --> 01:33:52,000
HOW ABOUT LADY RUNCORN?

1567
01:33:52,000 --> 01:33:55,000
IF HER NAME WAS BEATRICE
PALMERSTONE ONCE

1568
01:33:55,000 --> 01:33:58,000
THE CIGARETTE CASE
COULD HAVE BEEN HERS.

1569
01:33:58,000 --> 01:33:59,000
"B.P."

1570
01:33:59,000 --> 01:34:02,000
MAYBE SHE WAS WOING
WITH BERNARD PARKER.

1571
01:34:02,000 --> 01:34:05,000
WHAT ABOUT THAT
WRETCHED TRAMP?

1572
01:34:05,000 --> 01:34:07,000
JUST DON'T SEE
WHERE HE FITS IN.

1573
01:34:07,000 --> 01:34:10,000
MISS LEMON--
LOOK, OVER THERE.

1574
01:34:10,000 --> 01:34:13,000
IT'S HIM.

1575
01:34:13,000 --> 01:34:14,000
WAIT HERE.

1576
01:34:14,000 --> 01:34:17,000
HEY YOU, STOP!

1577
01:34:17,000 --> 01:34:22,000
CAPTAIN HASTINGS,
HE'S GOT A GUN!

1578
01:34:27,000 --> 01:34:28,000
ARE YOU ALL RIGHT?

1579
01:34:28,000 --> 01:34:30,000
I THINK SO.

1580
01:34:30,000 --> 01:34:32,000
( car engine starts )

1581
01:34:32,000 --> 01:34:36,000
LISTEN!

1582
01:34:36,000 --> 01:34:38,000
WELL, THAT'S
THE FIRST TRAMP I'VE SEEN

1583
01:34:38,000 --> 01:34:42,000
BEHIND THE WHEEL
OF AN M.G.!

1584
01:34:42,000 --> 01:34:44,000
( Hastings cries out in pain )

1585
01:34:44,000 --> 01:34:46,000
IT'S IODINE,
CAPTAIN HASTINGS.

1586
01:34:46,000 --> 01:34:48,000
I SAID IT'D STING.

1587
01:34:48,000 --> 01:34:50,000
IT IS ME WHO SUFFERS,
MY FRIEND.

1588
01:34:50,000 --> 01:34:52,000
TO THINK THAT I SENT YOU.

1589
01:34:52,000 --> 01:34:54,000
OH, IT'S NOT
YOUR FAULT, POIROT.

1590
01:34:54,000 --> 01:34:55,000
BUT YES, HASTINGS.

1591
01:34:55,000 --> 01:34:58,000
IT WAS ME WHO
SENT YOU TO THE HOUSE

1592
01:34:58,000 --> 01:35:00,000
AND BUT FOR YOUR
SPEED AND AGILITY...

1593
01:35:00,000 --> 01:35:02,000
HE FIRED
FOUR TIMES,
MR. POIROT!

1594
01:35:02,000 --> 01:35:04,000
YES, I KNOW, MISS LEMON.

1595
01:35:04,000 --> 01:35:07,000
DID YOU KNOW THE TRAMP
WOULD COME BACK?

1596
01:35:07,000 --> 01:35:08,000
NO, BUT I AM
AN IMBECILE--

1597
01:35:08,000 --> 01:35:10,000
I SEE ONLY HALF
OF THE PICTURE.

1598
01:35:10,000 --> 01:35:12,000
I DON'T EVEN
SEE THAT.

1599
01:35:12,000 --> 01:35:14,000
MISS LEMON,
WOULD YOU BE SO GOOD

1600
01:35:14,000 --> 01:35:17,000
AS TO TELEPHONE
THE CHIEF INSPECTOR JAPP.

1601
01:35:17,000 --> 01:35:19,000
ASK HIM TO MEET US

1602
01:35:19,000 --> 01:35:21,000
AT THE HOME
OF MARCUS HARDMAN.

1603
01:35:21,000 --> 01:35:24,000
WHAT ABOUT
YOUR LUNCH WITH
THE COUNTESS?

1604
01:35:24,000 --> 01:35:27,000
WE WILL MEET
AFTER LUNCH AT 3:00.

1605
01:35:27,000 --> 01:35:29,000
WHERE ARE YOU TAKING HER?

1606
01:35:29,000 --> 01:35:34,000
IT IS THE COUNTESS
WHO TAKES ME.

1607
01:35:41,000 --> 01:35:43,000
YOU ASTONISH ME, MADAME.

1608
01:35:43,000 --> 01:35:46,000
I AM LOST IN ADMIRATION.

1609
01:35:46,000 --> 01:35:48,000
IT'S JUST A PICNIC.

1610
01:35:48,000 --> 01:35:53,000
AH, WE USED TO HAVE
SUCH PICNICS!

1611
01:36:01,000 --> 01:36:04,000
SUPPOSE HE...

1612
01:36:04,000 --> 01:36:08,000
POIROT AND THE COUNTESS?

1613
01:36:08,000 --> 01:36:13,000
HE WOULDN'T...

1614
01:36:13,000 --> 01:36:15,000
WOULD HE?

1615
01:36:15,000 --> 01:36:17,000
I DON'T KNOW.

1616
01:36:17,000 --> 01:36:22,000
I'VE NEVER SEEN HIM
LIKE THIS BEFORE.

1617
01:36:25,000 --> 01:36:27,000
AND NOW A TOAST.

1618
01:36:27,000 --> 01:36:29,000
I SHALL PROPOSE ONE,
IF I MAY?

1619
01:36:29,000 --> 01:36:31,000
TO CRIME.

1620
01:36:31,000 --> 01:36:36,000
THAT IS A STRANGE TOAST
COMING FROM A DETECTIVE.

1621
01:36:37,000 --> 01:36:39,000
AND YET IT IS MY RAISON D'ETRE.

1622
01:36:39,000 --> 01:36:42,000
MY LIFE IS RULED BY IT.

1623
01:36:42,000 --> 01:36:46,000
AND IT IS CRIME
THAT HAS BROUGHT US TOGETHER.

1624
01:36:46,000 --> 01:36:51,000
THEN-- TO CRIME.

1625
01:36:52,000 --> 01:36:54,000
( glasses clink )

1626
01:36:54,000 --> 01:36:56,000
Lemon:
HE COULDN'T!

1627
01:36:56,000 --> 01:37:01,000
HE JUST COULDN'T.

1628
01:37:02,000 --> 01:37:07,000
YOU'D BE OUT OF A SITUATION.

1629
01:37:08,000 --> 01:37:09,000
WHAT ABOUT YOU?

1630
01:37:09,000 --> 01:37:11,000
OH, I COULD TRAVEL.

1631
01:37:11,000 --> 01:37:15,000
I'VE ALWAYS HAD A DREAM
ABOUT SOUTH AMERICA, YOU KNOW.

1632
01:37:15,000 --> 01:37:17,000
FARMING.

1633
01:37:17,000 --> 01:37:20,000
YOU'D MISS HIM THOUGH,
WOULDN'T YOU?

1634
01:37:20,000 --> 01:37:24,000
OF COURSE I WOULD.

1635
01:37:24,000 --> 01:37:29,000
WHAT ABOUT YOU?

1636
01:37:35,000 --> 01:37:40,000
I DON'T WANT
TO TALK ABOUT IT.

1637
01:37:42,000 --> 01:37:46,000
AND NOW I THINK... YES.

1638
01:37:46,000 --> 01:37:49,000
I HAVE AN
INVESTIGATION

1639
01:37:49,000 --> 01:37:52,000
WITH THE CHIEF
INSPECTOR JAPP.

1640
01:37:52,000 --> 01:37:55,000
AND IT MUST
BE RESOLVED.

1641
01:37:55,000 --> 01:37:57,000
IS THERE
ANYTHING I CAN DO?

1642
01:37:57,000 --> 01:38:01,000
THIS TIME I FEEL
THE CULPRIT MAY ESCAPE--

1643
01:38:01,000 --> 01:38:06,000
OR THIS TIME...
THE CULPRIT MAY NEVER BE NAMED.

1644
01:38:08,000 --> 01:38:11,000
THAT DEPENDS
ENTIRELY UPON YOU.

1645
01:38:11,000 --> 01:38:13,000
ME?

1646
01:38:13,000 --> 01:38:18,000
I THINK SO.

1647
01:38:24,000 --> 01:38:27,000
( rustling )

1648
01:38:27,000 --> 01:38:32,000
COULD THIS HELP?

1649
01:38:50,000 --> 01:38:52,000
Poirot:
ONLY FOUR PEOPLE

1650
01:38:52,000 --> 01:38:54,000
CAME INTO THE HOUSE
DURING THE CONCERT:

1651
01:38:54,000 --> 01:38:57,000
THE COUNTESS, MONSIEUR
JOHNSTONE, LADY RUNCORN

1652
01:38:57,000 --> 01:38:59,000
AND MONSIEUR PARKER.

1653
01:38:59,000 --> 01:39:00,000
A THOROUGH INVESTIGATION

1654
01:39:00,000 --> 01:39:04,000
ELIMINATED MONSIEUR JOHNSTONE
AND LADY RUNCORN--

1655
01:39:04,000 --> 01:39:07,000
MONSIEUR JOHNSTONE BECAUSE
HE IS A VERY WEALTHY MAN.

1656
01:39:07,000 --> 01:39:09,000
Japp:
AND LADY RUNCORN?

1657
01:39:09,000 --> 01:39:12,000
LADY RUNCORN BECAUSE...
SHE CAME TO THE HOUSE

1658
01:39:12,000 --> 01:39:16,000
IN ORDER TO MEET WITH
MONSIEUR BERNARD PARKER.

1659
01:39:16,000 --> 01:39:19,000
IS THAT NOT SO,
MONSIEUR HARDMAN?

1660
01:39:19,000 --> 01:39:20,000
YES.

1661
01:39:20,000 --> 01:39:22,000
BUT NO ONE MUST KNOW.

1662
01:39:22,000 --> 01:39:27,000
MY WHOLE BUSINESS--
IT THRIVES ON NOBODY KNOWING.

1663
01:39:27,000 --> 01:39:29,000
D'ACCORD.

1664
01:39:29,000 --> 01:39:31,000
AND FINALLY THERE
IS THE COUNTESS

1665
01:39:31,000 --> 01:39:35,000
WHO ENTERED THE HOUSE
IN ORDER TO GET HER COAT.

1666
01:39:35,000 --> 01:39:37,000
HER COAT?

1667
01:39:37,000 --> 01:39:39,000
I THOUGHT IT WAS A WARM EVENING.

1668
01:39:39,000 --> 01:39:41,000
YES, IT WAS, CHIEF INSPECTOR

1669
01:39:41,000 --> 01:39:44,000
BUT I HAD BEEN OUTSIDE
FOR MORE THAN AN HOUR.

1670
01:39:44,000 --> 01:39:46,000

YES, BUT...

1671
01:39:46,000 --> 01:39:50,000
IF THAT JAPANESE CONTRALTO
HAD SUNG ONE MORE HIGH NOTE

1672
01:39:50,000 --> 01:39:52,000
MY HEAD WOULD HAVE SHATTERED.

1673
01:39:52,000 --> 01:39:54,000
Poirot:
AND INSIDE,
YOU CAUGHT SIGHT

1674
01:39:54,000 --> 01:39:57,000
OF A FIGURE, NO?

1675
01:39:57,000 --> 01:39:59,000
AND YOU DISTURBED HIM,
N'EST-CE PAS?

1676
01:39:59,000 --> 01:40:01,000
YES, BUT I ONLY GLIMPSED HIM.

1677
01:40:01,000 --> 01:40:03,000
GOING WHERE?

1678
01:40:03,000 --> 01:40:06,000
FROM THIS ROOM TO THE WINDOW.

1679
01:40:06,000 --> 01:40:09,000
AH, YES, THE WINDOW
WITH THE CLIMBING PLANT.

1680
01:40:09,000 --> 01:40:11,000
THAT BRINGS US
TO THE TRAMP

1681
01:40:11,000 --> 01:40:15,000
AND ONE VERY
IMPORTANT QUESTION:

1682
01:40:15,000 --> 01:40:17,000
WHY DID HE COME BACK?

1683
01:40:17,000 --> 01:40:20,000
YES, AND HOW COULD
HE AFFORD AN M.G.?

1684
01:40:20,000 --> 01:40:24,000
THE TRAMP WHO WAS NO TRAMP
USED THE CLIMBING PLANT

1685
01:40:24,000 --> 01:40:26,000
IN ORDER TO ENTER
THE HOUSE.

1686
01:40:26,000 --> 01:40:29,000
NOW, FOR HIM THE WINDOW
WAS NO OBSTACLE.

1687
01:40:29,000 --> 01:40:31,000
THE SAFE ALSO
HE OPENED WITH EASE.

1688
01:40:31,000 --> 01:40:33,000
Japp:
SO WHAT ABOUT
THE CLUES?

1689
01:40:33,000 --> 01:40:35,000
THE GLOVE BELONGED
TO MONSIEUR PARKER.

1690
01:40:35,000 --> 01:40:38,000
IT WAS PUT THERE
TO INCRIMINATE HIM.

1691
01:40:38,000 --> 01:40:40,000
THE CASE BELONGED
TO LADY RUNCORN

1692
01:40:40,000 --> 01:40:43,000
OR BEATRICE PALMERSTONE,
AS SHE WAS CALLED.

1693
01:40:43,000 --> 01:40:46,000
BUT ALWAYS, I THINK,
SHE WILL DENY IT.

1694
01:40:46,000 --> 01:40:47,000
WHY?

1695
01:40:47,000 --> 01:40:50,000
SHE BROUGHT IT HERE
IN ORDER TO SELL IT.

1696
01:40:50,000 --> 01:40:52,000
SHE'LL NEVER
ADMIT THAT--
SHE'S TOO PROUD.

1697
01:40:52,000 --> 01:40:57,000
THAT IS QUITE TRUE, MISS LEMON.

1698
01:40:57,000 --> 01:40:59,000
SO THE TRAMP TOOK THE JEWELS.

1699
01:40:59,000 --> 01:41:01,000
WHAT DID HE DO THEN?

1700
01:41:01,000 --> 01:41:03,000
HE RETURNED TO THE WINDOW

1701
01:41:03,000 --> 01:41:06,000
WHERE HE WAS DISTURBED
BY THE COUNTESS.

1702
01:41:06,000 --> 01:41:08,000
HE SLIPPED AND HE FELL.

1703
01:41:08,000 --> 01:41:11,000
AND THE NECKLACE?

1704
01:41:11,000 --> 01:41:13,000
THE NECKLACE HE DROPPED

1705
01:41:13,000 --> 01:41:18,000
WHICH IS WHY HE
HAD TO COME BACK.

1706
01:41:20,000 --> 01:41:25,000
WAIT A MINUTE!

1707
01:41:49,000 --> 01:41:54,000
I'VE GOT IT!

1708
01:41:59,000 --> 01:42:01,000
VOILA!

1709
01:42:01,000 --> 01:42:02,000
GREEN AGAINST GREEN.

1710
01:42:02,000 --> 01:42:07,000
MY NECKLACE!

1711
01:42:08,000 --> 01:42:10,000
Japp:
WHAT ABOUT
THE TRAMP?

1712
01:42:10,000 --> 01:42:13,000
I'D STILL LIKE
TO COLLAR HIM, YOU KNOW.

1713
01:42:13,000 --> 01:42:15,000
OUI, D'ACCORD.

1714
01:42:15,000 --> 01:42:20,000
BUT I FEAR
IT IS TOO LATE.

1715
01:42:52,000 --> 01:42:54,000
Lemon:
WHAT'S THE MATTER?

1716
01:42:54,000 --> 01:42:57,000
JUST SOMETHING SHE SAID.

1717
01:42:57,000 --> 01:42:59,000
WHO?

1718
01:42:59,000 --> 01:43:01,000
THE COUNTESS.

1719
01:43:01,000 --> 01:43:03,000
SHE SAID THE SINGER
WAS JAPANESE.

1720
01:43:03,000 --> 01:43:06,000
IT WAS THE PIANIST
WHO WAS JAPANESE.

1721
01:43:06,000 --> 01:43:07,000
WHAT OF IT?

1722
01:43:07,000 --> 01:43:09,000
WELL, IF SHE
REALLY HAD BEEN

1723
01:43:09,000 --> 01:43:11,000
IN THE GARDEN
FOR AN HOUR

1724
01:43:11,000 --> 01:43:16,000
SURELY SHE WOULD
HAVE KNOWN.

1725
01:43:26,000 --> 01:43:29,000
GOOD NIGHT.

1726
01:43:29,000 --> 01:43:32,000
MISS LEMON, I THOUGHT
YOU HAD ALREADY GONE.

1727
01:43:32,000 --> 01:43:37,000
NO, I HAD SOME
LETTERS TO FINISH.

1728
01:43:39,000 --> 01:43:42,000
YOU'RE WORKING LATE,
MR. POIROT.

1729
01:43:42,000 --> 01:43:45,000
YES, I HAVE FROM
THE CHIEF INSPECTOR JAPP

1730
01:43:45,000 --> 01:43:48,000
THE REPORTS
OF THE JEWELRY THEFTS.

1731
01:43:48,000 --> 01:43:50,000
THIS WORK, MISS LEMON

1732
01:43:50,000 --> 01:43:54,000
SOMETIMES IT IS NOT
SO STRAIGHTFORWARD.

1733
01:43:54,000 --> 01:43:57,000
YOU DO WORK SO HARD,
MR. POIROT.

1734
01:43:57,000 --> 01:43:59,000
SOMETIMES YOU OUGHT...

1735
01:43:59,000 --> 01:44:01,000
YES, MISS LEMON?

1736
01:44:01,000 --> 01:44:04,000
NOTHING, MR. POIROT.

1737
01:44:04,000 --> 01:44:07,000
GOOD NIGHT, MISS LEMON.

1738
01:44:07,000 --> 01:44:12,000
GOOD NIGHT.

1739
01:44:32,000 --> 01:44:37,000
( squeal of brakes )

1740
01:44:41,000 --> 01:44:44,000
Poirot:
TEN MINUTES
UNTIL YOUR TRAIN.

1741
01:44:44,000 --> 01:44:46,000
Countess:
YES.

1742
01:44:46,000 --> 01:44:49,000
AND THEN YOUR SHIP
TO AMERICA.

1743
01:44:49,000 --> 01:44:52,000
YES.

1744
01:44:52,000 --> 01:44:57,000
THANK YOU.

1745
01:45:04,000 --> 01:45:06,000
MADAME...

1746
01:45:06,000 --> 01:45:10,000
YOU ARE THE MOST REMARKABLE,
THE MOST UNIQUE WOMAN

1747
01:45:10,000 --> 01:45:12,000
THAT I HAVE
EVER MET.

1748
01:45:12,000 --> 01:45:14,000
BUT ALSO...

1749
01:45:14,000 --> 01:45:15,000
OPPOSITES.

1750
01:45:15,000 --> 01:45:18,000
C'EST CA.

1751
01:45:18,000 --> 01:45:21,000
YOU MUST CONTINUE
YOUR WORK

1752
01:45:21,000 --> 01:45:24,000
AND I MUST
CONTINUE MINE.

1753
01:45:24,000 --> 01:45:29,000
BUT NOT IN THE SAME COUNTRY.

1754
01:45:36,000 --> 01:45:39,000
( train whistle blows )

1755
01:45:39,000 --> 01:45:41,000
COUNTESS,
PLEASE ALLOW ME

1756
01:45:41,000 --> 01:45:44,000
TO INTRODUCE TWO
COLLEAGUES OF MINE

1757
01:45:44,000 --> 01:45:46,000
MESSIEURS REDFERN
AND BLAKE.

1758
01:45:46,000 --> 01:45:48,000
GOOD EVENING,
MA'AM.

1759
01:45:48,000 --> 01:45:52,000
THEY ARE, LIKE MYSELF,
PRIVATE DETECTIVES.

1760
01:45:52,000 --> 01:45:54,000
DO YOU REALLY THINK
I NEED AN ESCORT?

1761
01:45:54,000 --> 01:45:57,000
NO, NO, NO, THEY ARE
FOR YOUR PROTECTION.

1762
01:45:57,000 --> 01:46:00,000
DURING YOUR SHORT
VISIT IN ENGLAND

1763
01:46:00,000 --> 01:46:03,000
THERE HAVE BEEN FOUR
VERY CLEVER ROBBERIES.

1764
01:46:03,000 --> 01:46:06,000
I WOULD NOT WISH YOU TO
BE INVOLVED IN A FIFTH.

1765
01:46:06,000 --> 01:46:08,000
OF COURSE.

1766
01:46:08,000 --> 01:46:13,000
( train whistle blows )

1767
01:46:15,000 --> 01:46:17,000
ARE THEY ARMED?

1768
01:46:17,000 --> 01:46:21,000
I BELIEVE THAT MONSIEUR
REDFERN DOES CARRY A GUN.

1769
01:46:21,000 --> 01:46:23,000
YES, LOADED WITH
BLANKS, OF COURSE.

1770
01:46:23,000 --> 01:46:26,000
AH, YES, MONSIEUR
PLAYED THE TRAMP

1771
01:46:26,000 --> 01:46:29,000
MORE CONVINCINGLY
THAN I HAD EXPECTED.

1772
01:46:29,000 --> 01:46:31,000
BUT PAUVRE HASTINGS.

1773
01:46:31,000 --> 01:46:34,000
I AM AFRAID THAT
IT WAS NECESSARY

1774
01:46:34,000 --> 01:46:39,000
TO LEAD HIM
OFF THE SCENT.

1775
01:47:00,000 --> 01:47:03,000
( whistle blows )

1776
01:47:03,000 --> 01:47:05,000
BUT I FORGET!

1777
01:47:05,000 --> 01:47:08,000
I HAVE SOMETHING
FOR YOU.

1778
01:47:08,000 --> 01:47:12,000
A PARTING PRESENT?

1779
01:47:12,000 --> 01:47:15,000
A SOUVENIR.

1780
01:47:15,000 --> 01:47:16,000
THE CIGARETTE CASE?

1781
01:47:16,000 --> 01:47:20,000
THE DOUBLE CLUE.

1782
01:47:20,000 --> 01:47:24,000
BUT IT'S
OF NO USE TO ME--

1783
01:47:24,000 --> 01:47:27,000
IT'S INSCRIBED "B.P."

1784
01:47:27,000 --> 01:47:31,000
YES, AND YET IN
THE RUSSIAN ALPHABET

1785
01:47:31,000 --> 01:47:36,000
A "B" IS A "V" AND A "P"
IS AN "R," IS IT NOT?

1786
01:47:36,000 --> 01:47:41,000
VERA ROSSAKOFF?

1787
01:47:45,000 --> 01:47:49,000
YOU'VE BEEN STUDYING.

1788
01:47:49,000 --> 01:47:51,000
YOU WILL KEEP IT,
I HOPE?

1789
01:47:51,000 --> 01:47:54,000
SOMETHING TO
REMEMBER ME BY.

1790
01:47:54,000 --> 01:47:59,000
OH, I WON'T FORGET YOU...

1791
01:48:03,000 --> 01:48:07,000
HERCULE.

1792
01:48:07,000 --> 01:48:12,000
NOR I YOU...

1793
01:48:12,000 --> 01:48:16,000
COUNTESS.

1794
01:48:16,000 --> 01:48:21,000
( blows whistle )

1795
01:48:25,000 --> 01:48:30,000
AU REVOIR!

1796
01:48:54,000 --> 01:48:58,000
VERA ROSSAKOFF WASN'T THE ONLY
WOMAN OF THE ARISTOCRACY

1797
01:48:58,000 --> 01:49:00,000
TO STOOP TO JEWEL THEFT.

1798
01:49:00,000 --> 01:49:02,000
DURING THE REIGN OF LOUIS XVI

1799
01:49:02,000 --> 01:49:05,000
A COUNTESS DISCOVERED
THAT THE CARDINAL OF PARIS

1800
01:49:05,000 --> 01:49:08,000
WAS IN LOVE
WITH MARIE ANTOINETTE.

1801
01:49:08,000 --> 01:49:12,000
SHE CONVINCED HIM THAT THE QUEEN
RETURNED HIS AFFECTIONS.

1802
01:49:12,000 --> 01:49:15,000
AS PROOF, SHE GAVE HIM
A LETTER FROM MARIE URGING HIM

1803
01:49:15,000 --> 01:49:19,000
TO PURCHASE A PRICELESS
DIAMOND NECKLACE FOR HER.

1804
01:49:19,000 --> 01:49:22,000
THE LETTER, OF COURSE,
WAS A CLEVER FORGERY

1805
01:49:22,000 --> 01:49:23,000
BUT THE CARDINAL READ IT

1806
01:49:23,000 --> 01:49:26,000
LESS WITH HIS EYES
THAN HIS HEART

1807
01:49:26,000 --> 01:49:28,000
AND HE BELIEVED EVERY WORD.

1808
01:49:28,000 --> 01:49:30,000
AT A MIDNIGHT RENDEZVOUS
IN A PALACE GARDEN

1809
01:49:30,000 --> 01:49:33,000
THE CARDINAL MET
A HEAVILY-VEILED WOMAN

1810
01:49:33,000 --> 01:49:35,000
HE THOUGHT WAS
HIS BELOVED QUEEN.

1811
01:49:35,000 --> 01:49:38,000
HE OFFERED HER THE NECKLACE
WITH A FLOURISH

1812
01:49:38,000 --> 01:49:42,000
AND IN A WHISPER SHE PROMISED
TO REWARD HIM WITH HER FAVORS.

1813
01:49:42,000 --> 01:49:45,000
AS SHE DISAPPEARED
INTO THE NIGHT

1814
01:49:45,000 --> 01:49:48,000
THE CARDINAL SIGHED IN ECSTACY.

1815
01:49:48,000 --> 01:49:50,000
BUT THE WHOLE AFFAIR
BLEW UP IN HIS FACE

1816
01:49:50,000 --> 01:49:53,000
WHEN THE JEWELER
WHO SOLD HIM THE NECKLACE

1817
01:49:53,000 --> 01:49:56,000
PRESENTED HIS BILL
DIRECTLY TO THE QUEEN.

1818
01:49:56,000 --> 01:50:00,000
MARIE WAS SO ANGRY
AT THE CARDINAL FOR HIS FOLLY

1819
01:50:00,000 --> 01:50:02,000
SHE TOSSED HIM
INTO THE BASTILLE.

1820
01:50:02,000 --> 01:50:05,000
THE COUNTESS, IN THE MEANTIME,
ESCAPED TO LONDON

1821
01:50:05,000 --> 01:50:09,000
WHERE SHE AND HER HUSBAND
LIVED IN GRAND STYLE

1822
01:50:09,000 --> 01:50:11,000
OFF THE SPOILS OF THEIR CRIME.

1823
01:50:11,000 --> 01:50:15,000
AS FOR THE UNFORTUNATE CARDINAL,
HE WAS RELEASED FROM PRISON

1824
01:50:15,000 --> 01:50:19,000
BUT THE QUEEN-- STILL FURIOUS--
BANISHED HIM INTO EXILE

1825
01:50:19,000 --> 01:50:21,000
SURELY AN UNJUST FATE

1826
01:50:21,000 --> 01:50:25,000
FOR A MAN WHOSE ONLY CRIME
WAS TO LOSE HIS HEART.

1827
01:50:25,000 --> 01:50:28,000
PERHAPS HE TOOK CONSOLATION
SOME YEARS LATER

1828
01:50:28,000 --> 01:50:30,000
WHEN MARIE LOST HER HEAD.

1829
01:50:30,000 --> 01:50:32,000
I'M DIANA RIGG.

1830
01:50:32,000 --> 01:50:37,000
GOOD NIGHT.

1831
01:51:50,000 --> 01:51:55,000
CAPTIONS COPYRIGHT LONDON
WEEKEND TELEVISION INTERNATIONAL

1832
01:51:59,000 --> 01:52:04,000
CHRISTIE CAME FROM A SOLIDLY
UPPER-MIDDLE-CLASS BACKGROUND

1833
01:52:04,000 --> 01:52:06,000
BUT INTERESTINGLY

1834
01:52:06,000 --> 01:52:10,000
THE WAY THAT SHE PROJECTS
HER DETECTIVE FICTION

1835
01:52:10,000 --> 01:52:13,000
THERE'S A SORT OF TWIST
PUT ON HER RELATIONSHIP

1836
01:52:13,000 --> 01:52:16,000
WITH UPPER-MIDDLE-CLASS
BRITISH SOCIETY

1837
01:52:16,000 --> 01:52:19,000
BECAUSE THE PERSON WHO
IS INTERROGATING IT

1838
01:52:19,000 --> 01:52:22,000
THE PERSON WHO IS QUESTIONING IT

1839
01:52:22,000 --> 01:52:25,000
IS THIS STRANGE
LITTLE BELGIAN PERSON.

1840
01:52:25,000 --> 01:52:29,000
POIROT DOESN'T FIT IN--
PHYSICALLY HE DOESN'T FIT IN.

1841
01:52:29,000 --> 01:52:33,000
IF YOU COMPARE THE TALL, TWEEDY,
SLIM, HATTED HASTINGS

1842
01:52:33,000 --> 01:52:36,000
WITH THIS OBSESSIVELY NEAT

1843
01:52:36,000 --> 01:52:38,000
EXTRAORDINARILY
FORMALLY-DRESSED--

1844
01:52:38,000 --> 01:52:41,000
THE SPATS, THE TIE,
THE WHOLE LOOK--

1845
01:52:41,000 --> 01:52:44,000
HE DOESN'T FIT IN
TO THE ENGLISH SCENE

1846
01:52:44,000 --> 01:52:48,000
AND THEREFORE HE IS ABLE
TO PICK THINGS OUT OF IT.

1847
01:52:48,000 --> 01:52:51,000
HE'S ABLE TO QUESTION
WHAT THE BRITISH--

1848
01:52:51,000 --> 01:52:55,000
PARTICULARLY THE PUKKA BRITISH,
THE PUBLIC SCHOOL TYPES

1849
01:52:55,000 --> 01:52:58,000
PEOPLE LIKE HASTINGS WHO'VE
GONE TO THE RIGHT SCHOOLS

1850
01:52:58,000 --> 01:53:00,000
WHO'VE GONE TO
THE RIGHT UNIVERSITY

1851
01:53:00,000 --> 01:53:03,000
WHO JOINED THE RIGHT REGIMENT--

1852
01:53:03,000 --> 01:53:05,000
YOU KNOW, IT'S
CAPTAIN HASTINGS--

1853
01:53:05,000 --> 01:53:09,000
WHO'VE HAD ALL THE RIGHT MALE
BACKGROUND, IN A CERTAIN SENSE--

1854
01:53:09,000 --> 01:53:12,000
HE'S ABLE TO SAY,
"OKAY, THAT IS TRUE

1855
01:53:12,000 --> 01:53:16,000
"BUT THAT DOESN'T MEAN THESE
PEOPLE ARE BEYOND REPROACH.

1856
01:53:16,000 --> 01:53:19,000
"IT DOES NOT MEAN THAT
THEY TOO CANNOT BE TEMPTED.

1857
01:53:19,000 --> 01:53:23,000
IT DOES NOT MEAN THAT THEY TOO
CANNOT COMMIT FOUL CRIMES."

1858
01:53:23,000 --> 01:53:27,000
AND IN A NUMBER OF INSTANCES
IT IS IN FACT THIS VERY PUKKA

1859
01:53:27,000 --> 01:53:30,000
THIS EXTREMELY,
THIS BEYOND REPROACH MALE

1860
01:53:30,000 --> 01:53:32,000
THIS WHITE MALE

1861
01:53:32,000 --> 01:53:35,000
WHO IS, IN FACT,
THE VILLAIN OF THE PIECE.

1862
01:53:35,000 --> 01:53:37,000
IN THAT SENSE, I THINK

1863
01:53:37,000 --> 01:53:41,000
POIROT IS A REFLECTION OF
CHRISTIE'S POSITION AS A WOMAN

1864
01:53:41,000 --> 01:53:45,000
WHO IS DE-CENTERED FROM
THE MAIN POLITICAL-SOCIAL SCENE

1865
01:53:45,000 --> 01:53:49,000
WHO IS, TO SOME EXTENT, PART OF
IT-- YOU KNOW, HASTINGS AND HE

1866
01:53:49,000 --> 01:53:52,000
LIVE A VERY UPPER-
MIDDLE-CLASS BRITISH LIFE

1867
01:53:52,000 --> 01:53:54,000
IN THEIR BEAUTIFUL APARTMENT

1868
01:53:54,000 --> 01:53:57,000
BUT AT THE SAME TIME
THEY'RE NOT QUITE OF IT.

1869
01:53:57,000 --> 01:54:01,000
POIROT IS A SLIGHTLY DE-CENTERED
AND SO ABLE TO MAKE ANALYSES.

1870
01:54:01,000 --> 01:54:04,000
NOW, WHEN WE COME TO HIS
RELATIONSHIP WITH JAPP--

1871
01:54:04,000 --> 01:54:08,000
JAPP IS ANOTHER PART
OF THE BRITISH SPECTRUM.

1872
01:54:08,000 --> 01:54:11,000
HE IS THE SOLID,
LOWER-MIDDLE-CLASS MAYBE

1873
01:54:11,000 --> 01:54:15,000
EVEN WORKING-CLASS BRITISH
PERSON WITH A NON-PUKKA ACCENT--

1874
01:54:15,000 --> 01:54:18,000
HE AND HASTINGS
DO NOT SOUND THE SAME--

1875
01:54:18,000 --> 01:54:21,000
JAPP CLEARLY DID NOT GO
TO THE RIGHT SCHOOL

1876
